},{"ratio":0.82,"watchRatio":0.48995},{"ratio":0.83,"watchRatio":0.48665},{"ratio":0.84,"watchRatio":0.48505000000000004},{"ratio":0.85,"watchRatio":0.48255000000000003},{"ratio":0.86,"watchRatio":0.48085},{"ratio":0.87,"watchRatio":0.47615},{"ratio":0.88,"watchRatio":0.4712},{"ratio":0.89,"watchRatio":0.46699999999999997},{"ratio":0.9,"watchRatio":0.46515},{"ratio":0.91,"watchRatio":0.45840000000000003},{"ratio":0.92,"watchRatio":0.4538},{"ratio":0.93,"watchRatio":0.4476},{"ratio":0.94,"watchRatio":0.44494999999999996},{"ratio":0.95,"watchRatio":0.43979999999999997},{"ratio":0.96,"watchRatio":0.43235},{"ratio":0.97,"watchRatio":0.4235},{"ratio":0.98,"watchRatio":0.41125},{"ratio":0.99,"watchRatio":0.39725},{"ratio":1,"watchRatio":0.3913}],"script":{"source_path":"all-146-tagged.json","words":131,"wpm":null,"lang":"en","chapters":[],"blocks":[],"text":"Did you know that [music] the BCCI's logo isn't actually Indian? Most fans think that the BCCI's emblem is a proud national symbol, but its origin [music] is British and hiding in plain sight. The design comes directly from the colonial medal called as [music] the Most Exalted Order of the Star of India. Queen Victoria created it in 1861 right after the 1857 revolt to reward the Indian princes who stayed loyal to the crown. Put the medal next to the BCCI logo, it's almost identical. When the BCCI was formed in 1928 under the British rule, they simply adopted the Imperial emblem and after independence, it was never replaced. So, every time our Indian players walk onto the cricket field, our jerseys still remind us of the British Raj."}}